  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nA cold front moving across northeast WI this afternoon was \nproducing a band of thick clouds. As the front passes, skies\nwill clear with mostly sunny skies expected. Tranquil conditions\nprevail tonight with a preview of fall like temperatures with lows\nin the 40s to around 50. The expected fgen band for tonight will \npass to the south of the area. On Sunday, per coordination with \nsurrounding offices to the north and west, lowered high temperatures\na few degrees, especially across the north based on the latest \nMET/MAV guidance. This will mean high temperatures will be mainly \nin the 60s on Sunday, with a few lower 70s across east-central \nWI. For Sunday night, high pressure centered across the area \ncombined with light winds and clear skies should bring the first \nwidespread frost of the season across the north. Some of our \ncolder spots could drop to or a few degrees below freezing for a \ncouple of hours around sunrise. \n\nStrong low pressure across the northern plains will move northeast\ninto Canada with a trailing cold front approaching Wisconsin on \nMonday. Clouds will be on the increase with rain arriving \nduring the afternoon for locations mainly west of the Fox Valley. \nThis in response to an increasing 850mb low level jet and warm \nadvection. Meanwhile, a wave of low pressure will move along the \nfront Monday night as it moves across the region. Precipitable \nwater near 2\" are near the top of the charts for this time of \nyear. Some complexities to the forecast as the NBM probabilities\nover an inch are 50-80% from central into northeast WI while LREF\nprobabilities are in the 20-50%. Highest probabilities in both\nsolutions are out towards Wausau and Wisconsin Rapids. The risk\nof severe weather appears to stay just to our south. Any lingering\nshowers should end Tuesday morning across northeast WI. \n\nThe boundary should far enough south for Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning for dry conditions. Beyond that, there could be\na few rounds of showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms Wednesday\nafternoon through Thursday. By Friday, the Canadian/ECMWF have a \nwetter solution compared to the GFS. Below normal temps for Sunday \ninto Monday, then seasonable temps for the rest of the work week.",
